          <h2>A hilltop town with tighter streets</h2>
          <p>Dudley's older streets, particularly the ones climbing towards the castle and the town centre, are narrower and steeper than the newer parts of town, and parking can be limited at busy times. If your car breaks down near the castle or in the older part of town, let us know as much as you can about the street width, the gradient and any parking restrictions nearby — it helps us bring the right vehicle and plan how to load safely.</p>

          <h2>On the M5 into the Black Country</h2>
          <p>Most of the Dudley breakdowns we attend on the open road happen on the M5, which passes close to the town on its way south-west from the M6 interchange near West Bromwich, through the Black Country and on towards Worcester. This stretch carries a steady mix of commuter and haulage traffic, with the usual motorway hazards — if your vehicle starts to falter, ease off early and aim for the nearest hard shoulder, slip road or verge rather than trying to press on.</p>

          <h2>A market town with a genuine hill</h2>
          <p>Unlike most of the flatter Birmingham conurbation, Dudley sits on a limestone ridge, and the roads up towards the castle and zoo are noticeably steeper than anything nearby. That matters for recovery — a car that's struggled to climb the hill, or one that's rolled back on a gradient, needs a slightly different approach than a straightforward flat-road breakdown, so let us know if you're on a hill when you call.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>Broken down on the hill near the castle?</strong> If you can, apply the handbrake fully and turn your wheels towards the kerb before leaving the vehicle. Keep hazards on, stay well clear of traffic on the narrower stretches, and let us know it's the hill so we can bring suitable equipment.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

